{
  "case_id": "2008-08-495",
  "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear\nargument first this morning in Nijhawan v. Holder.\nMr. Moseley."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Thomas E. Moseley",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Since Congress required conviction, the\ntime-honored categorical approach really should be the\ngoverning standard, and I submit that there's nothing in\nthe plain language of the statute, the underlying\nstatute enacted by Congress, to oust that time-honored\napproach, which I submit is perhaps on a par as being\npresumptively applicable, similarly to the -- to the\nstay standards that this Court discussed very recently\nin the Nken case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, in that connection,\nat some point in the argument -- and you may be a little\nearly because you're talking about the statute -- I'd like to know either anecdotally from your experience or\n-- or because it's written somewhere, how often do\njuries give special verdicts? It actually applies in\nthe second case we're to hear as well. And has that\nchanged in the light of -- of Apprendi? In -- in my\nexperience, we just didn't know many of the features of\nthe crime from -- from the jury verdict, and I just\nwould like to know if that's changed in this day and\nage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: And if you -- if you do\nextend that lifeline to the government, aren't you\nconceding that the amount of the loss is not an element\nof the offense, and aren't you conceding that it is not\nnecessary for the loss amount to be an element of the\noffense?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Did you have any authority for the -- the idea that a trial judge in a criminal case should ask the jury to answer a special interrogatory regarding a question that has no bearing on the conviction, but may have a bearing on the future immigration status of the defendant, which is what you're suggesting should be done in these tax cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But I can see that if there are multiple defendants in the case, some of the defendants might say that this is unnecessary, it's inflammatory."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government (Mr. Moseley)",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOSELEY: What -- what I am suggesting in the tax case is that it would be perfectly appropriate for the government to seek such a special interrogatory if they wish to establish the tax loss, which is an element of the -- which is a necessary component of the offense, if they wanted to establish it -- if they wanted to establish it for -- for purposes -- for whatever purpose they wanted to establish it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: And -- and why would you be reluctant to do that? Would you just spell that out a little bit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GANNON: Yes, that it -- this is the offense of conviction. This is just like the domestic relationship prong of the misdemeanor crime of domestic violence that the Court decided in its recent decision in United States v Hayes, that -- that there is a prior conviction, some aspects of which were elements of the underlying offense, and in order to establish whether the prior conviction needs the statutory definition in the subsequent proceeding, the government will need to bear the appropriate burden of proof for that proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the last three years, according to sentencing commission data, there isn't a single defendant in the 1326 proceeding who received a sentence of more than 10 years and had an increase on the basis of an aggravated felony that would include the category that we're dealing with here in subparagraph (M)(1)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Moseley",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MOSELEY: With respect to the issue of deference, the fact that this is part of a Federal criminal statute I believe doesn't get us -- cuts off the inquiry, and we don't get to Chevron deference here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2008-08-495",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9e21b2335b17ecd78011cd230ca973f55890f5a7a2fd7db10f90a12c41ce4192",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9e21b2335b17-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GANNON: The court of appeals should be affirmed."
    },
    {
